  "data_abstract": "The strong Coulomb field created in ultrarelativistic heavy ion collisions is expected to produce a rapidity-dependent difference ($\\Delta v_2$) in the second Fourier coefficient of the azimuthal distribution (elliptic flow, $v_2$) between $D^0$ ($\\overline{u}c$) and $\\overline{D}^0$ ($u\\overline{c}$) mesons. Motivated by the search for evidence of this field, the CMS detector at the LHC is used to perform the first measurement of $\\Delta v_2$. The rapidity-averaged value is found to be $<\\Delta v_2>=0.001\\pm0.001(stat)\\pm0.003(syst)$ in PbPb collisions at $\\sqrt{s_{NN}}=5.02~TeV$. In addition, the influence of the collision geometry is explored by measuring the $D^0$ and $\\overline{D}^0$ mesons $v_2$ and triangular flow coefficient ($v_3$) as functions of rapidity, transverse momentum ($p_T$), and event centrality (a measure of the overlap of the two Pb nuclei). A clear centrality dependence of prompt $D^0$ meson $v_2$ values is observed, while the $v_3$ is largely independent of centrality. These trends are consistent with expectations of flow driven by the initial-state geometry.",
  "data_keywords": {
